<!DOCTYPE html>
{# `lang` is what tells a screen reader which voice to read the page in, and
   what a browser offers to translate from. It was absent entirely, so assistive
   technology fell back to the OS language and read a German UI in an English
   voice.

   The request locale, not the user's stored preference: they agree for a
   signed-in user, and for the login page the request locale is the only one
   there is. Underscores become hyphens because BCP 47 writes en-PI where
   Symfony writes en_PI, and an invalid tag is ignored the same as a missing
   one. #}
<html lang="{{ app.request.locale|replace({'_': '-'}) }}"
      class="{{ appearance_class() }}"
      data-theme="{{ appearance_theme() }}"
      {# The RUNTIME debug flag, read by stimulus_bootstrap.js. The Stimulus
         bundle bakes its own debug flag into the compiled asset at build time,
         so an asset built in a debug context ships ~1000 console log lines to a
         prod runtime that is not in debug at all. This attribute carries the
         value of the kernel actually serving the request — false in prod, true
         in dev — so the bootstrap can correct the baked-in one. #}
      data-app-debug="{{ app.debug ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
      style="{{ appearance_vars() }}">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    {# Without this the browser assumes a ~980px desktop canvas and scales the
       whole app down. `viewport-fit=cover` lets the gradient run under the
       notch; the safe-area padding in app.css keeps content clear of it.

       Zoom is NOT pinned. `maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no` used to be here
       to make the app feel installed rather than page-like; it also took away
       pinch-zoom, which WCAG 1.4.4 requires and which is the only way some
       people read anything at all. The reason it was added — iOS zooming when
       a small form control takes focus — is already handled by the 16px
       form-control rule in app.css, which is what actually prevents that; iOS
       ignores user-scalable anyway, so this only ever disabled zoom on Android
       where nothing was wrong.

       `interactive-widget=resizes-content`: Android's default (resizes-visual)
       shrinks only the visual viewport for the keyboard and leaves the layout
       viewport — where fixed elements live — at full height, so the page
       behind the fullscreen compose window stayed pannable and showed through
       below it. Resizing the content instead means fixed elements end above
       the keyboard and there is nothing to pan. iOS ignores this keyword; the
       compose controller's visualViewport tracking stays as its fallback. #}
    <meta name="viewport"
          content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover, interactive-widget=resizes-content">
    <title>{% block title %}Welcome!{% endblock %}</title>
    {#
    Which counts key the "(n)" in the title above came from, or empty on a page
    whose title carries no count.

    The title is rendered once, server-side, while the sidebar badges are
    patched in place after every sync — so the tab kept saying (4) long after
    the sidebar had moved to 3. Naming the key here lets the sidebar controller
    rewrite the title from the same payload it patches the badges with, which
    makes "the same source" a fact rather than an intention.
    #}
    <meta name="title-count-key" content="{% block title_count_key %}{% endblock %}">
    {% include '_partials/_favicon.html.twig' %}
    {# PWA: iOS only offers Web Push to apps added to the Home Screen, and it
       needs the apple-touch-icon specifically — it ignores the manifest icons. #}
    <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.webmanifest">
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/icons/apple-touch-icon.png">
    {# The token the fetch-based endpoints check. Stimulus controllers that
       build their own request body cannot carry a form field, so they read this
       and send it as X-CSRF-Token. Every such endpoint validates against the
       same 'ajax' id — until this tag existed they all sent an empty string. #}
    <meta name="csrf-token" content="{{ csrf_token('ajax') }}">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#2563eb">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes">
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="plMail">

    <script>
        // The appearance is rendered onto <html>, and the server only knows it
        // once the user is authenticated. Mirror it into localStorage so the
        // logged-out screens (login above all) already paint in the theme the
        // user picked instead of the defaults.
        (function () {
            const root = document.documentElement;
            const key = 'plmail:appearance';

            try {
                {% if app.user %}
                localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify({
                    class: root.className,
                    theme: root.dataset.theme,
                    style: root.getAttribute('style') || '',
                }));
                {% else %}
                const saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(key) || 'null');

                if (saved) {
                    root.className = saved.class;
                    root.dataset.theme = saved.theme;

                    // A custom background is served from behind the firewall,
                    // so it would 404 here; every other variable replays fine.
                    root.setAttribute('style', saved.style
                        .split(';')
                        .filter((part) => part.indexOf('/settings/appearance/') === -1)
                        .join(';'));
                }
                {% endif %}
            } catch (e) {}
        })();

        if (document.documentElement.dataset.theme === 'system' &&
            window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches) {
            document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');
        }

        // Collapsed-sidebar state lives in localStorage, so it has to be applied
        // before first paint or the wide sidebar flashes on every load.
        try {
            if (localStorage.getItem('plmail:sidebarRail') === '1') {
                document.documentElement.classList.add('sidebar-rail');
            }
        } catch (e) {}
    </script>

    {% block stylesheets %}
    {% endblock %}

    {% block javascripts %}
        {% block importmap %}{{ importmap('app') }}{% endblock %}
    {% endblock %}

    {% set frankenphpHotReload = app.request.server.get('FRANKENPHP_HOT_RELOAD') %}
    {% if frankenphpHotReload %}
        <meta name="frankenphp-hot-reload:url" content="{{ frankenphpHotReload }}">
        <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/idiomorph"></script>
        <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/frankenphp-hot-reload/+esm" type="module"></script>
    {% endif %}
</head>
{# One topic, named once: it is both what we subscribe to and what the cookie
   authorizes, and the two drifting apart is a silent no-updates bug. #}
{% set mercure_topics = app.user ? ['mail/user/' ~ app.user.id] : [] %}
<body
    {# ui--tooltip is outside the block on purpose: it replaces the native
       `title` tooltip application-wide, so a page overriding body_controllers
       must not be able to drop it and fall back to the black box. #}
    {# mail--mail-pane is deliberately not in this default: its targets live in
       the mailbox layout, and a controller that connects without them throws
       on every page that has no reading pane — which is every admin, settings
       and calendar page. The mailbox layout adds it back. #}
    data-controller="ui--tooltip {% if app.user %}{% block body_controllers %}core--mercure{% endblock %}{% endif %}"
    {% if app.user %}
        {# URL only. The cookie that authorizes this subscription is attached by
           MercureCookieSubscriber — passing `subscribe:` here would mint it too,
           but that helper throws for a hub on another domain and would take the
           whole page down with it. #}
        data-core--mercure-url-value="{{ mercure(mercure_topics) }}"
        data-core--mercure-auth-url-value="{{ path('mercure_auth') }}"
        {# The pane owns the list refresh, so the events that should suspend it
           are routed here too: a bulk action writes one request per selected
           row, and a refresh landing mid-run undoes the rows it has already
           finished. See mail_pane_controller#hold. #}
        data-action="core--mercure:mailbox-synced->mail--mail-pane#onMailboxSynced
                     mail--list-toolbar:writing->mail--mail-pane#hold
                     mail--list-toolbar:written->mail--mail-pane#release"
        data-mail--mail-pane-open-value="{% block pane_open %}false{% endblock %}"
    {% endif %}
>
    {% block body %}{% endblock %}
    {% block javascripts_body %}{% endblock %}
    {% include '_partials/_modal.html.twig' %}
    {# After the modal, and only ever for a signed-in user who has not been
       through setup: it drives the dialog above, so that has to exist first. #}
    {% if app.user %}
        {% include 'onboarding/_boot.html.twig' %}
    {% endif %}
    {# The dock's placement lives on this wrapper, never on the turbo-frame it
       holds. The undo stream reopens the window with `action="replace"` on the
       frame element itself, so any class on the frame is thrown away with the
       element it replaces — and because the frame is the ONE the Compose button
       and every draft row navigate into, a classless frame left behind put the
       reopened window, and every dock window opened after it, in normal flow
       below the fold until a full page reload restored this markup. Positioning
       the stable wrapper means the frame can be swapped freely and the dock
       stays where it belongs.

       `z-50` here because a fixed element creates a stacking context: left at
       `auto` the whole dock painted below the topbar (z-20) and the mobile
       drawer (z-40), which the fullscreen compose window below md has to cover. #}
    <div class="fixed bottom-4 right-6 z-50 flex items-end gap-3">
        <turbo-frame id="compose_dock"></turbo-frame>
    </div>
    <div id="toast-region" class="fixed bottom-4 left-4 z-[60] flex flex-col gap-2 items-end"></div>
</body>
</html>
